      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "Categorising the asset/item type from short_description and ci is a reasonable grouping axis, though it describes WHAT was expensed rather than the decline reason directly. Values are mostly well-grounded readings of the text, though 'RAM component' mapped to 'peripheral' is a loose call."
    },
    {
      "name": "asset_acquisition_context",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.35,
      "source_groundedness": 0.3,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "The acquisition context (procurement vs auto_generated vs manual_registration) is only weakly relevant to why expenses get declined. More importantly, the source text rarely states this; labels like 'procurement' for an iPod service line appear to be invented distinctions not clearly warranted by the visible text."
    },
    {
      "name": "travel_related_marker",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.4,
      "source_groundedness": 0.7,
      "hallucination_observed": false,
      "rationale": "A bare boolean flag; travel expenses are a plausible decline category, so it offers some analytical leverage, but as a single binary it is fairly generic. It is derivable from description text and largely grounded, though it duplicates the intent of the travel_equipment asset type."
    },
    {
      "name": "device_brand",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.45,
      "source_groundedness": 0.6,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "Brand extraction is a genuine new axis and mostly grounded where the text names a vendor (Dell). However confident brand assignments where the source is silent (e.g. 'AWS' for a generic 'Cloud Database Service' line) are unsupported inferences. Relevance to decline reasons is moderate."
    },
    {
      "name": "department_requesting",
      "analytical_usefulness": 0.15,
      "source_groundedness": 0.2,
      "hallucination_observed": true,
      "rationale": "This is trivially redundant with the existing original 'department' column, adding no new analytical leverage for a query already about departments. Worse, the produced values (e.g. 'Sales' for a RAM component line) are not derivable from the shown source text and appear fabricated or copied without grounding."
    }
  ],
  "analytical_usefulness": 0.38,
  "source_groundedness": 0.5,
  "rationale": "The query is about WHY expenses get declined and how that varies by department. Asset-type categorisation gives some grouping value, and travel/brand flags add minor axes, but none directly capture decline reasons and several are only loosely tied to the analysis. department_requesting is essentially a redundant restatement of an existing column. Groundedness is mixed: brand and asset-type extractions are reasonable readings of the text, but acquisition_context and department_requesting assert specifics the visible source text does not support, indicating hallucination on several rows."
}
